## The change under test, and why this one

Run 1's own dynamic change: **move vlan48's address from `10.38.22.66/27` to
`10.38.22.70/27`** while the device is operating. Run 1 established that
`ip address A.B.C.D/M` on an SVI that already has one **replaces it in place** -- no error, no
secondary -- and that **the whole L3 identity moves with it, including the source address of
that interface's own ICMP unreachables**.

That last part is what makes this limb gradeable on the wire with no ambiguity. One
`arpleg.py` leg sends ICMP to **both** addresses from `eth4` and the answer is a matched pair:

- the address that **exists** answers **type 0 code 0**, an echo reply, from itself;
- the address that **does not** draws **type 3 code 1**, host unreachable, **sourced from
  whichever address vlan48 currently holds** -- and that source is the discriminator.

So each leg carries its own control: whichever of the two is the subject, the other must NOT
answer, and the unreachable's source address says which one the DUT thinks it is.

```
sudo arpleg.py --tag <tag> --out /tmp/ntp1021/T47192 --iface eth4 \
     --icmp 10.38.22.66,10.38.22.70 \
     --sender-ip 10.38.22.65 --sender-mac 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f \
     --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 4 --wait 4
```
(`--gw-mac` is the DUT's SVI MAC; every SVI on this platform shares one, `platforms/x960.md`
§6.5, so the same value addresses either SVI.)

## The limb

### LEG A -- baseline, vlan48 is `.66` (tag `t192-base`)

```
  cap t192-base-eth4.pcap: 19 frame(s)
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .66 answers
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.70   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t3/c1 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .70 unreachable, FROM .66
    x3   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.70 tell 10.38.22.66
```
`.66` answers 4 of 4; `.70` does not exist and the DUT ARPs for it from `.66`.

### LEG B -- the change applied WHILE OPERATING (tag `t192-changed`)

```
awplus(config)#interface vlan48
awplus(config-if)#ip address 10.38.22.70/27          [box 04:52:55 UTC]
awplus#show ip interface brief
vlan48   10.38.22.70/27   admin up  running
```
No reload, no service restart, no `write` yet.
```
  cap t192-changed-eth4.pcap: 21 frame(s)
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.70   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.70 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .70 now answers
    x4   ICMP t3/c1 10.38.22.70 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .66 unreachable, FROM .70
    x3   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.66 tell 10.38.22.70
```
**The exact mirror image of leg A, in every one of the five rows.** The DUT now answers for
`.70`, declares `.66` unreachable, sources that unreachable from `.70`, and ARPs for `.66` --
the address it used to own. The change took effect dynamically; this is run 1's result
reproduced as this run's own control.

### LEG C -- the limb: after a cold boot, nothing typed (tag `t192-afterboot`)

```
awplus#write
Building configuration...
[OK]
awplus#show startup-config | include 10.38.22
ip address 10.38.22.34/27
 ip address 10.38.22.70/27          <- the dynamic change, saved
 ip address 10.38.22.97/27

tools/tb ckreload tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 240
17:53:40 >>> reload   (04:53:40 UTC)
reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
17:54:41 login seen after 60 s
```

🔬 **The leg was settled before it was read** -- the lesson from T33380 run 2 and T33386 run 2
earlier the same hour. The first post-boot `ping` to `.70` returned **1 of 4, 75% packet loss**;
the second returned **4 of 4**. Only then was the leg taken.

```
  cap t192-afterboot-eth4.pcap: 22 frame(s)
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.70   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.70 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .70 still answers
    x4   ICMP t3/c1 10.38.22.70 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .66 unreachable, FROM .70
    x3   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.66 tell 10.38.22.70
    x1   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.70 tell 10.38.22.70       (duplicate-address probe at boot)
    x1   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.65 tell 10.38.22.70
```
**Row for row identical to leg B**, taken 22 s after the login prompt with **nothing typed into
the DUT**. The change made dynamically survived the restart complete with its side effects --
the unreachable source address is still `.70`, so the whole L3 identity was restored, not just
the address line.

The extra `who-has 10.38.22.70 tell 10.38.22.70` is the SVI's own duplicate-address probe at
boot, which is itself evidence that the interface was brought up from the saved configuration
rather than left over.

